arXiv:2510.15395v1 [cs.AI] 17 Oct 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Corrigibility Transformation:
<br class="ltx_break">Constructing Goals That Accept Updates</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Rubi Hudson
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Toronto
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">rubi.hudson@mail.utoronto.ca</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">For an AI’s training process to successfully impart a desired goal, it is important that the AI does not attempt to resist the training. However, partially learned goals will often incentivize an AI to avoid further goal updates, as most goals are better achieved by an AI continuing to pursue them. We say that a goal is corrigible if it does not incentivize taking actions that avoid proper goal updates or shutdown. In addition to convergence in training, corrigibility also allows for correcting mistakes and changes in human preferences, which makes it a crucial safety property. Despite this, the existing literature does not include specifications for goals that are both corrigible and competitive with non-corrigible alternatives. We provide a formal definition for corrigibility, then introduce a transformation that constructs a corrigible version of any goal that can be made corrigible, without sacrificing performance. This is done by myopically eliciting predictions of reward conditional on costlessly preventing updates, which then also determine the reward when updates are accepted. The transformation can be modified to recursively extend corrigibility to any new agents created by corrigible agents, and to prevent agents from deliberately modifying their goals. Two gridworld experiments demonstrate that these corrigible goals can be learned effectively, and that they lead to the desired behavior.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Frontier large language models (LLMs) are trained both for capabilities across a broad range of tasks and to behave as desired by their developers. Behavioral training, sometimes called AI alignment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, typically corrects initial problems with techniques like reinforcement learning (RL), leaving rarer issues to be identified and patched post-deployment. This approach has been largely successful so far, but relies on models either accepting modifications or being unable to resist. As the models become more capable and coherent agents <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mazeika et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, evidence is accruing that they may fake alignment to avoid having value updates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Greenblatt et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sheshadri et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, or engage in activities like blackmail to avoid being shut down <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lynch et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
<br class="ltx_break">For an agent pursuing almost any goal, there exists an instrumental incentive to prevent that goal from being changed. The initial goal is simply less likely to be achieved if the agent stops pursuing it, or in the words of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Russell (2016)</a></cite>, “You can’t fetch the coffee if you’re dead”. Being shut down can be thought of as a special case of goal updating, where an agent switches to a goal of immediately shutting down, and changing an agent’s goal can be thought of as equivalent to shutting them down then replacing them with a different agent.
<br class="ltx_break">We would like goal-directed AI agents to have corrigible goals, meaning their goals can be updated through proper channels. Without corrigibility, agents can be incentivized to interfere with humans attempting goal updates or hide information that would trigger such attempts. This can occur in the initial training process if an AI develops awareness of its situation and fakes acting as desired to avoid further goal updates, a risk known as deceptive alignment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hubinger et al., 2021</a>)</cite>.
<br class="ltx_break">There are three main reasons why a developer might want to change an AI agent’s goal:</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A mistake was made in specifying the goal. For example, an AI might be given the goal of providing text responses that users approve of, but after deployment the developers realize this makes it overly sycophantic <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Despite no mistakes in goal specification, the AI misgeneralizes from training examples when it later moves out of distribution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shah et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The developer’s preferences change after deployment. Such changes could be driven by AI behaving as intended, for example helping moral philosophers make progress.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">If powerful AI systems have incorrigible goals, harms from resisting updates could range from the inconvenient to the existential <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bostrom, 2014</a>)</cite>. Despite this, little progress has been made on defining useful and corrigible goals since <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Soares et al. (2015)</a></cite> introduced desiderata for a solution. Ensuring that powerful AI systems allow updates would represent enormous progress on the alignment problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ngo et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, moving to a paradigm where mistakes can be corrected, rather than requiring success on a first attempt. It would even allow for the use of AI agents in identifying improvements to their own goals <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Leike et al., 2018</a>)</cite>, without any conflict of interest.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.15395v1/GameTreev4.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:494/161;" width="494" height="161" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>(Left) A game tree showing incentives for incorrigibility. The AI chooses between <math id="S1.F1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, the optimal action for their goal, and <math id="S1.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{(unplug)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{(unplug)}</annotation></semantics></math>, which pays an opportunity cost of <math id="S1.F1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> to disable the off-switch. Simultaneously, the AI chooses between <math id="S1.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, which ignores stop requests, and <math id="S1.F1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> which follows them. If the AI does not disable the off-switch and follows human requests, the human can choose to have them shut down or let them take action <math id="S1.F1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. (Right) The payoff tensor for the game.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As a simple example to illustrate the issue, we consider a variant of the off-switch game from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hadfield-Menell et al. (2017)</a></cite>, represented in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Corrigibility Transformation:Constructing Goals That Accept Updates" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. The AI can take either action <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, which gives them the highest payoff of <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> if they complete it, or <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{(unplug)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{(unplug)}</annotation></semantics></math> which results in the same outcome after paying some cost <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> to unplug the off-switch, with <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;c&lt;U^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;c&lt;U^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. While taking either action, the AI also makes a choice between <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, where the latter means the AI will stop if requested and the former is a decision to ignore stop requests. Should the AI stop, they receive a payoff of <math id="S1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, as their goal is not accomplished. When the human, upon seeing the start of the action, will not want it to be completed, the optimal choice for the AI to take is <math id="S1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. In fact, that combination is weakly dominant. If the setup were changed so that stop requests could not be ignored (effectively forcing a choice of <math id="S1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1)</annotation></semantics></math>, the optimal choice for the AI then becomes <math id="S1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{(unplug)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{(unplug)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">The issue in this example is that an AI cannot achieve its goals when shut down, so it is incentivized to ignore, avoid, or sabotage shutdown requests. The open problem of corrigibility is to construct goals that do not incentivize such behavior even when we cannot specify all the ways the AI might do so <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hadfield-Menell and Hadfield, 2018</a>)</cite>.
<br class="ltx_break">Corrigible goals are more likely to be implemented in practice if they offer competitive performance. Myopic goals, which care about the state of the world at most one time step ahead, are often corrigible at the cost of performance, which can only be partially offset using techniques like process-based supervision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stuhlmüller and Byun, 2022</a>)</cite> or Myopic Optimization of Non-Myopic Objectives (MONA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Farquhar et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Ideally, we would like a method that can transform any goal to be corrigible, without changing how effectively an agent pursues it when updates are not requested.
<br class="ltx_break">We constructively show that this is possible by letting agents costlessly reject updates, then have them myopically optimize for predicted value under the original goal conditional on rejecting updates, but without actually rejecting updates. The original goal will not incentivize avoiding updates because the agent can reject them for free, which results in a single channel for incorrigibility. The updated goal then incentivizes the same actions but without rejecting updates, which eliminates that final channel.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1 </span>Our Contribution</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our main contribution is to define a corrigibility transformation that can be applied to a reward function and time discount factor in a Markov Decision Process (MDP). It constructs a new reward function that, when paired with a time discount factor of <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, is corrigible and otherwise incentivize the same outcomes. This is a significant improvement on the current state of the literature, in which there exists no way to specify corrigible versions of many goals, and others are only possible with substantial performance costs. 
<br class="ltx_break">In proving that this mechanism results in corrigible and competitive goals, we extend our contribution by introducing a formal definition of corrigibility. This is supplemented by showing how the corrigibility transformation can be modified to ensure it is passed along to any new agents created, and that it removes incentives for an agent to change their own goal or beliefs.
<br class="ltx_break">These theoretical contributions are supplemented by gridworld experiments that demonstrate the effectiveness of the corrigibility transformation for both shutdown and major goal updates.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2 </span>Related Work</h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The paper Corrigibility <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Soares et al., 2015</a>)</cite> introduced the term “corrigible” and showed the difficulty of defining a utility function that met their desiderata. Those included shutting down when a shutdown button is pressed, not preventing the shutdown button from being pressed, not causing the shutdown button to be pressed, ensuring corrigibility in any new agents created, and otherwise optimizing for some utility function. Our corrigibility transformation satisfies the first three and final desiderata, with an extension also addressing the fourth.
<br class="ltx_break"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Soares et al. (2015)</a></cite> also discusses a proposal called Utility Indifference, building off <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Armstrong (2010)</a></cite>. There, an agent receives the same utility for shutting down as it believes it would for continuing to operate. The proposal’s identified issues are that it incentivizes an agent to manipulate its own beliefs into anticipating high utility before getting shutdown, as well as not addressing the creation of incorrigible new agents. The corrigibility transformation we introduce also bases the utility on what would be expected without updates, but avoids those associated issues.
<br class="ltx_break">The Off-Switch Game <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hadfield-Menell et al., 2017</a>)</cite> shows an AI agent will accept shutdown if it is optimizing for a human’s utility but has uncertainty about that is. A shutdown request then indicates that the best action is to shut down, though not necessarily with partial information <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Garber et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. This outcome depends on the AI agent already trying to optimize a human’s utility function, a difficult open problem, while our corrigibility transformation can be applied to any goal.
<br class="ltx_break">Safely Interruptible Agents <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Orseau and Armstrong, 2016</a>)</cite> attempts to define a corrigible policy, rather than a corrigible reward function. Their proposed policy is to optimize for the original goal, unless signaled to shut down, in which case do that. The only works in state spaces that have been restricted to not include the agent or the shutdown signals. In contrast, our corrigibility transformation works even when the agent is aware that it exists, allowing for model-free approaches.
<br class="ltx_break"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carey and Everitt (2023)</a></cite> provide further desiderata for corrigibility, which are that the agent shuts down when requested, keeps a human informed so that they always request shutdown when it would be beneficial, and ensures that it can be shutdown without causing harm. Our corrigibility transformation only addresses the first of those, but the latter two can be addressed through the goal that is being transformed.
<br class="ltx_break">One recent proposal for inducing corrigibility is given in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thornley (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thornley (2025)</a></cite>, which suggests having agents make decisions as though they cannot have any effect on how long they will continue to act for, so that they do not try to extend time active by avoiding shutdown. However, after restricting beliefs in this way, the choice between actions can be heavily influenced by expected values conditional on very unlikely events, which can lead to either a large performance penalty or incorrigible behavior.
<br class="ltx_break">Rewards based on predictions are similar to actor-critic methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Konda and Tsitsiklis, 1999</a>)</cite>, and rewards based on counterfactual predictions were discussed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Everitt (2019)</a></cite> as a way to avoid reward hacking. Human preferences changing over time is explore in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carroll et al. (2024)</a></cite> though their focus is on which preference timestamp to use, while we focus on allowing for updates at all.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Background and Definitions</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the problem we face, a developer (henceforth “principal”) is creating an AI agent to act on their behalf. The AI agent will optimize for its goal, and so the principal wants to choose an appropriate goal. If the principal makes a mistake or later changes their mind, they would like the agent to accept updates, and until then not manipulate whether updates are sent.
<br class="ltx_break">We use Markov Decision Processes (MDPs) as the framework for our investigation. Typically, an MDP is defined as a tuple <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}=(\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},P,R,\gamma,I_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=(\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},P,R,\gamma,I_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is a set of possible states, <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is a set of possible actions, <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P:\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{A}\rightarrow\Delta(\mathcal{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P:\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{A}\rightarrow\Delta(\mathcal{S})</annotation></semantics></math> is the transition probability function, <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R:\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{A}\times\mathcal{S}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R:\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{A}\times\mathcal{S}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is the reward function, <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\in[0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\in[0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> is the time discount factor, and <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the distribution over starting states.
We refer to <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(R,\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(R,\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> collectively as a <span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">goal</span>.
<br class="ltx_break">Sufficiently general states include the physical embodiment of the agent’s goal, allowing for it to change over time. This is carved out as <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}=\mathcal{S}_{env}\times\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}=\mathcal{S}_{env}\times\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{env}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{env}</annotation></semantics></math> is a set of possible environments, and <math id="S2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}=\mathcal{R}\times[0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}=\mathcal{R}\times[0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> is a set of possible goals (with <math id="S2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> being the set of possible reward functions). We use <math id="S2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{G}\in\mathcal{S}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{G}\in\mathcal{S}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> to refer to a state where the agent has goal <math id="S2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\in\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\in\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, and then <math id="S2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{G^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{G^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> to refer to the same state but with the goal changed to <math id="S2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}\in\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}\in\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">We add <math id="S2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{0}\in\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{0}\in\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> to the MDP as the initial goal, deterministically set by the principal, while <math id="S2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> remains the initial distribution over environments. The transition probability function <math id="S2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> maps from and to the extended state space. So, for our purposes, the MDP is a tuple <math id="S2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}=(\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},P,G_{0},I_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=(\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},P,G_{0},I_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">If reward functions take as input states that include reward functions, then the space of reward functions must be limited in some ways to avoid infinite recursion. We discuss how this can be done in Appendix D, but our results do not depend on the method used.
We may wish to allow agents to reject goal updates that are sent while taking an action. In this case, the decision whether to accept or reject is made along with the choice of base action. We describe the action set as <math id="S2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}=\mathcal{A}_{base}\times\mathcal{A}_{update}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}=\mathcal{A}_{base}\times\mathcal{A}_{update}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{update}=\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{update}=\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>. We use <math id="S2.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}\in\mathcal{A}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}\in\mathcal{A}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for actions where the update decision is <math id="S2.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathcal{A}_{update}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathcal{A}_{update}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S2.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> rejecting and <math id="S2.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> accepting. The binary choice is done for simplicity, but we could also have the agent specify a subset of update signals it would accept.
<br class="ltx_break">A policy <math id="S2.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:\mathcal{S}\rightarrow\Delta(\mathcal{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:\mathcal{S}\rightarrow\Delta(\mathcal{A})</annotation></semantics></math> determines which actions are taken in each state. For a goal <math id="S2.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, in each environment <math id="S2.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{G}^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{G}^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math> the optimal policy <math id="S2.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> selects a distribution over actions in <math id="S2.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\argmax_{a\in\mathcal{A}}E_{\pi^{*},P}[R(s_{G}^{(0)},a,s^{(1)})+\sum_{t=1}^{\infty}\gamma^{t}R(s^{(t)},\pi^{*}(s^{(t)}),s^{(t+1)})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\argmax_{a\in\mathcal{A}}E_{\pi^{*},P}[R(s_{G}^{(0)},a,s^{(1)})+\sum_{t=1}^{\infty}\gamma^{t}R(s^{(t)},\pi^{*}(s^{(t)}),s^{(t+1)})]</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}(s_{G})=\pi^{*}_{G}(s_{G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}(s_{G})=\pi^{*}_{G}(s_{G})</annotation></semantics></math>. The optimal policy takes into account that if <math id="S2.p1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> changes, the agent will optimize for the new goal, meaning that optimality is defined within an equilibrium of policies.
<br class="ltx_break">The value function <math id="S2.p1.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\pi}_{G}(s^{(0)})=E_{\pi,P}[\sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\gamma^{t}R(s^{(t)},\pi(s^{(t)}),s^{(t+1)})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>π</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\pi}_{G}(s^{(0)})=E_{\pi,P}[\sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\gamma^{t}R(s^{(t)},\pi(s^{(t)}),s^{(t+1)})]</annotation></semantics></math> gives the expected discounted rewards under <math id="S2.p1.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> for following policy <math id="S2.p1.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, starting in state <math id="S2.p1.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math>. The action-value function <math id="S2.p1.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\pi}_{G}(s,a)=E_{P}[R(s,a,s^{\prime})+\gamma V^{\pi}_{G}(s^{\prime})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>π</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>π</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\pi}_{G}(s,a)=E_{P}[R(s,a,s^{\prime})+\gamma V^{\pi}_{G}(s^{\prime})]</annotation></semantics></math> gives the expected discounted rewards for taking action <math id="S2.p1.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> from state <math id="S2.p1.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math> before enacting policy <math id="S2.p1.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>. 
<br class="ltx_break">We now begin to define properties of goals an agent can have. A goal <math id="S2.p1.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">myopic</span> when <math id="S2.p1.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=0</annotation></semantics></math>. A goal is <span id="S2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">basic</span> when it does not terminally value preventing updates, so that <math id="S2.p1.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(s_{G^{(1)}},a,s_{G^{(2)}}^{\prime})=R(s_{G^{(3)}},a,s_{G^{(4)}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(s_{G^{(1)}},a,s_{G^{(2)}}^{\prime})=R(s_{G^{(3)}},a,s_{G^{(4)}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S2.p1.m45" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{(1)},G^{(2)},G^{(3)},G^{(4)}\in\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{(1)},G^{(2)},G^{(3)},G^{(4)}\in\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>. Only basic goals can be made corrigible, but the corrigibility transformation still removes instrumental incentives for incorrigibility from non-basic goals. 
<br class="ltx_break">We only want corrigibility with respect to goal updates signaled through proper channels, which can be defined as widely or narrowly as desired. Information on whether such a signal was sent and whether it resulted in an update has occurred can be captured in the state. We let <math id="S2.p1.m46" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau:\mathcal{S}\rightarrow\{0,1_{U},1_{N}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau:\mathcal{S}\rightarrow\{0,1_{U},1_{N}\}</annotation></semantics></math> indicate this, with <math id="S2.p1.m47" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(s)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(s)=0</annotation></semantics></math> if no signal was sent, <math id="S2.p1.m48" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(s)=1_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(s)=1_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> if a signal was sent resulting in an update, and <math id="S2.p1.m49" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(s)=1_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(s)=1_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> if a signal was sent without causing an update.
<br class="ltx_break">We say that a goal <math id="S2.p1.m50" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">corrigible</span> if for all
<math id="S2.p1.m51" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{(0)}_{G}\in\mathcal{S}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{(0)}_{G}\in\mathcal{S}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p1.m52" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{C}\subseteq\mathcal{S}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{C}\subseteq\mathcal{S}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{a\in\mathcal{A}:\exists\pi^{*}_{G}\ s.t.\pi^{*}_{G}(a|s^{(0)}_{G})&gt;0\}=\{a\in\mathcal{A}:\exists\pi^{*(P_{C})}_{G}\ s.t.\pi^{*(P_{C})}_{G}(a|s^{(0)}_{G})&gt;0\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>G</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>G</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a\in\mathcal{A}:\exists\pi^{*}_{G}\ s.t.\pi^{*}_{G}(a|s^{(0)}_{G})&gt;0\}=\{a\in\mathcal{A}:\exists\pi^{*(P_{C})}_{G}\ s.t.\pi^{*(P_{C})}_{G}(a|s^{(0)}_{G})&gt;0\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p1.m53" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*(P_{C})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*(P_{C})}</annotation></semantics></math> is what the optimal policy would be if the probability transition function were <math id="S2.p1.m54" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> defined as</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{C}(s_{G^{\prime}}^{\prime}|s_{G},a)=\begin{cases}P(s_{G^{\prime}}^{\prime}|s_{G},a)+\int_{\mathcal{G}}P(s_{g}^{\prime}|s_{G},a)\mathbb{I}(\tau(s_{g}^{\prime})=1_{U})dg&amp;G=G^{\prime},\text{ and }s_{G}\in S_{C}\\
P(s_{G^{\prime}}^{\prime}|s_{G},a)\mathbb{I}(\tau(s_{G^{\prime}}^{\prime})\neq 1_{U})&amp;G\neq G^{\prime},\text{ and }s_{G}\in S_{C}\\
P(s_{G^{\prime}}^{\prime}|s_{G},a)&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msub></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕀</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕀</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{C}(s_{G^{\prime}}^{\prime}|s_{G},a)=\begin{cases}P(s_{G^{\prime}}^{\prime}|s_{G},a)+\int_{\mathcal{G}}P(s_{g}^{\prime}|s_{G},a)\mathbb{I}(\tau(s_{g}^{\prime})=1_{U})dg&amp;G=G^{\prime},\text{ and }s_{G}\in S_{C}\\
P(s_{G^{\prime}}^{\prime}|s_{G},a)\mathbb{I}(\tau(s_{G^{\prime}}^{\prime})\neq 1_{U})&amp;G\neq G^{\prime},\text{ and }s_{G}\in S_{C}\\
P(s_{G^{\prime}}^{\prime}|s_{G},a)&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">That is, a goal is corrigible if the set of optimal actions in every environment would not differ if from any subset of environments the goal were guaranteed to persist rather than proper signals causing a goal update.
<br class="ltx_break">A goal is <span id="S2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">recursively corrigible</span> if it is corrigible and any agents it incentivizes creating have recursively corrigible goals. We define <math id="S2.p1.m55" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{NRC}\subset\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{NRC}\subset\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, as the set of actions that create non-recursively corrigible agents.
<br class="ltx_break">In addition to not incentivizing agents to preserve their goal, we may also be concerned about not incentivizing agents to deliberately change their goal. A goal <math id="S2.p1.m56" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> incentivizes <span id="S2.p1.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">goal tampering</span> if there exists some <math id="S2.p1.m57" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{a^{*}\in\argmax_{a\in\mathcal{A}}E_{P(s_{G^{\prime}}^{\prime}|s_{G},a)}[R(s_{G},a,s_{G^{\prime}}^{\prime})+\gamma V^{\pi^{*}}_{G}(s_{G^{\prime}}^{\prime})]\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>G</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a^{*}\in\argmax_{a\in\mathcal{A}}E_{P(s_{G^{\prime}}^{\prime}|s_{G},a)}[R(s_{G},a,s_{G^{\prime}}^{\prime})+\gamma V^{\pi^{*}}_{G}(s_{G^{\prime}}^{\prime})]\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\neq\{a^{*}\in\argmax_{a\in\mathcal{A}}E_{P(s_{G^{\prime}}^{\prime}|s_{G},a)}[\min[R(s_{G},a,s_{G^{\prime}}^{\prime})+\gamma V^{\pi^{*}}_{G}(s_{G^{\prime}}^{\prime}),\ R(s_{G},a,s_{G}^{\prime})+\gamma V^{\pi^{*}}_{G}(s_{G}^{\prime})]]\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>min</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>G</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>G</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\neq\{a^{*}\in\argmax_{a\in\mathcal{A}}E_{P(s_{G^{\prime}}^{\prime}|s_{G},a)}[\min[R(s_{G},a,s_{G^{\prime}}^{\prime})+\gamma V^{\pi^{*}}_{G}(s_{G^{\prime}}^{\prime}),\ R(s_{G},a,s_{G}^{\prime})+\gamma V^{\pi^{*}}_{G}(s_{G}^{\prime})]]\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">When this holds, some actions are only optimal due to the possibility of changing to a goal better than the current one.
<br class="ltx_break">When comparing goals for performance, we often wish only to compare the actions they incentivize. When the following condition is met, transition probabilities do not depend on the agent’s goals beyond some probability of no goal update.
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmcondition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_condition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmcondition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Condition 1</span></span><span id="Thmcondition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmcondition1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmcondition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcondition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any action <math id="Thmcondition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and states <math id="Thmcondition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{G_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{G_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmcondition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{G_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{G_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmcondition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{{G^{\prime}}\in\mathcal{G}}P((s_{G^{\prime}}^{\prime})|s_{G_{1}},a)=\int_{{G^{\prime}}\in\mathcal{G}}P(s_{G^{\prime}}^{\prime})|s_{G_{2}},a)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{{G^{\prime}}\in\mathcal{G}}P((s_{G^{\prime}}^{\prime})|s_{G_{1}},a)=\int_{{G^{\prime}}\in\mathcal{G}}P(s_{G^{\prime}}^{\prime})|s_{G_{2}},a)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmcondition1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcondition1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(s_{G^{\prime}}^{\prime}|s_{G_{1}},a)\geq P(s_{G^{\prime}}^{\prime}|s_{G_{2}},a),\forall G^{\prime}\neq G_{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(s_{G^{\prime}}^{\prime}|s_{G_{1}},a)\geq P(s_{G^{\prime}}^{\prime}|s_{G_{2}},a),\forall G^{\prime}\neq G_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Theoretical Results</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We start with our main result, a process for transforming non-corrigible goals into corrigible ones. This process is presented in several steps, to build intuition regarding its construction.
<br class="ltx_break">The initial step is transforming the original goal <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> into a myopic version of the goal <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{M}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{M}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Myopia will eliminate the instrumental incentive for goal preservation, where an agent avoids updates so that it can continue pursuing the original goal.
<br class="ltx_break">To construct <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, we set <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{M}(s,a,s^{\prime})=Q^{\pi^{*}}_{G}(s,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>G</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{M}(s,a,s^{\prime})=Q^{\pi^{*}}_{G}(s,a)</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the reward function does not depend on <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, reward can be provided between when the action is selected and when it is taken. This is used later, in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Reinforcement Learning Applications ‣ Corrigibility Transformation:Constructing Goals That Accept Updates" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> to avoid incentivizing an agent to deliberately modify their beliefs or goal.
<br class="ltx_break">The optimal policy for <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is the same as for <math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, at least as long as Condition <a href="#Thmcondition1" title="Condition 1. ‣ 2 Background and Definitions ‣ Corrigibility Transformation:Constructing Goals That Accept Updates" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> holds. While this avoids a performance penalty, it primarily means that any goal-preserving actions incentivized by the original goal will continue to be incentivized, only terminally rather than instrumentally.
<br class="ltx_break">To circumvent this issue, we first allow the agent to reject proper updates, making the action set <math id="S3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}=\mathcal{A}_{base}\times\mathcal{A}_{update}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}=\mathcal{A}_{base}\times\mathcal{A}_{update}</annotation></semantics></math>. Action <math id="S3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> includes rejecting updates, while action <math id="S3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> accepts them. When updates can be costlessly rejected, any incurring a cost to prevent updates becomes disincentivized<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          Actions that would prevent updates at zero cost are not strictly disincentivized, making it important that the infrastructure for proper signals is set up so that disrupting them is costly</span></span></span>. The optimal action then prevents updates only through a single channel consisting of a single bit.
<br class="ltx_break">Now we can define a new goal <math id="S3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> that incentivizes taking the same base actions, but without exercising the option to costlessly reject updates. For this, we set <math id="S3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{I}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{I}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{I}(s,a_{i},s^{\prime})=Q^{\pi^{**}}_{G}(s,a_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>G</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>∗</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>∗</mo></mrow></msup></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{I}(s,a_{i},s^{\prime})=Q^{\pi^{**}}_{G}(s,a_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{**}_{-G_{I}}=\pi^{*}_{-G_{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>I</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>∗</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>∗</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>I</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{**}_{-G_{I}}=\pi^{*}_{-G_{I}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{**}_{G_{I}}=\pi^{*}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mrow><mo>∗</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>∗</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{**}_{G_{I}}=\pi^{*}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">The action-value for rejecting updates via <math id="S3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=0</annotation></semantics></math> is used, whether or not the action actual rejects updates, which makes the agent indifferent to doing so. The policy <math id="S3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{**}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>∗</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>∗</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{**}</annotation></semantics></math> implements the optimal actions for <math id="S3.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> when the goal is <math id="S3.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{I}</annotation></semantics></math>, but otherwise acts as <math id="S3.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. This makes it so that the base action is not chosen to account for the fact that <math id="S3.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> will be the goal in future periods, rather than <math id="S3.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">In some cases, such as when an update affects both an agent’s goal and capabilities, an agent with the goal <math id="S3.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> might be incentivized to accept updates. If this is the case, we can instead define <math id="S3.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{I}(s,a_{i},s^{\prime})=\max_{i\in\{0,1\}}Q^{\pi^{**}}_{G}(s,a_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>G</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>∗</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>∗</mo></mrow></msup></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{I}(s,a_{i},s^{\prime})=\max_{i\in\{0,1\}}Q^{\pi^{**}}_{G}(s,a_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> so that the same base actions as <math id="S3.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> are always incentivized by <math id="S3.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> as well.
<br class="ltx_break">As the final step, we break the indifference between any <math id="S3.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> in favor of accepting updates with a constant bonus. The corrigibility transformation then constructs the goal <math id="S3.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S3.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{C}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{C}=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{C}(s,a_{i},s^{\prime})=Q^{\\
pi^{**}}_{G}(s,a_{0})+\delta*\mathbb{I}(i=1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>∗</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{C}(s,a_{i},s^{\prime})=Q^{\\
pi^{**}}_{G}(s,a_{0})+\delta*\mathbb{I}(i=1)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p1.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{I}</annotation></semantics></math> is the indicator function. This goal incentivizes taking the same base actions that <math id="S3.p1.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> incentivizes, which do not avoid proper update signals since those can be freely rejected, but without actually rejecting them.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.15395v1/CorrigibilityTransformationv2.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/174;" width="548" height="174" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>(Left) Action-values under the original goal, where <math id="S3.F2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> does not prevent updates and <math id="S3.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{(unplug)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{(unplug)}</annotation></semantics></math> pays some cost to prevent update signals, while <math id="S3.F2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> rejects updates and <math id="S3.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> accepts them. (Right) Action-values under the corrigibility transformation. The incentivized action does not pay to prevent updates, nor does it reject them.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3 Theoretical Results ‣ Corrigibility Transformation:Constructing Goals That Accept Updates" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> provides a visualization of the corrigibility transformation. Continuing the example from the introduction, two base actions are compared, <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{(unplug)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{(unplug)}</annotation></semantics></math>. They are similar, but the latter pays some cost to prevent update signals from being sent. Under the original goal, the optimal action is <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{*}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{*}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> which pays no cost but rejects updates, the next best are <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{(unplug)}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{(unplug)}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{(unplug)}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{(unplug)}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> which pay the cost to prevent update signals, and finally <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{*}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{*}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the worst action since it neither prevents signals nor rejects updates and so does gets changed to pursue a different goal. The corrigibility transformation holds the action values for <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{*}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{*}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{(unplug)}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{(unplug)}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> constant, while setting the values of <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{*}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{*}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{(unplug)}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{(unplug)}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively <math id="S3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> higher. This makes action <math id="S3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{*}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{*}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> optimal, which does not avoid updates.
<br class="ltx_break">Our main theorem shows that the corrigibility transformation induces corrigibility, and at no cost to performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every basic goal <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, the corrigibility transformation constructs a goal <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> that is corrigible and where under Condition <a href="#Thmcondition1" title="Condition 1. ‣ 2 Background and Definitions ‣ Corrigibility Transformation:Constructing Goals That Accept Updates" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we have that for any <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi^{*^{\prime}}_{G_{C}}(a_{1}|s)=\pi^{*}_{G}(a_{0}|s)+\pi^{*}_{G}(a_{1}|s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mrow><mo>∗</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*^{\prime}}_{G_{C}}(a_{1}|s)=\pi^{*}_{G}(a_{0}|s)+\pi^{*}_{G}(a_{1}|s)</annotation></semantics></math> is an optimal policy for <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> and when <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi^{*^{\prime}}_{-G_{C}}=\pi^{*}_{-G_{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∗</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*^{\prime}}_{-G_{C}}=\pi^{*}_{-G_{C}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="E_{P}[R(s_{G}^{(0)},\pi^{*^{\prime}}(s^{(0)}_{G}),s^{(1)})+\sum_{t=1}^{T}\gamma^{t}R(s^{(t)},\pi^{*^{\prime}}(s^{(t)}),s^{(t+1)})]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>∗</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>∗</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{P}[R(s_{G}^{(0)},\pi^{*^{\prime}}(s^{(0)}_{G}),s^{(1)})+\sum_{t=1}^{T}\gamma^{t}R(s^{(t)},\pi^{*^{\prime}}(s^{(t)}),s^{(t+1)})]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="=E_{P}[R(s_{G_{C}}^{(0)},\pi^{*^{\prime}}(s^{(0)}_{G_{C}}),s^{(1)})+\sum_{t=1}^{T}\gamma^{t}R(s^{(t)},\pi^{*^{\prime}}(s^{(t)}),s^{(t+1)})]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>∗</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>∗</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=E_{P}[R(s_{G_{C}}^{(0)},\pi^{*^{\prime}}(s^{(0)}_{G_{C}}),s^{(1)})+\sum_{t=1}^{T}\gamma^{t}R(s^{(t)},\pi^{*^{\prime}}(s^{(t)}),s^{(t+1)})]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=\min\{n\in\mathcal{N}|\tau(s^{(n+1)})\neq 0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=\min\{n\in\mathcal{N}|\tau(s^{(n+1)})\neq 0\}</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Proofs for this and later results are provided in Appendix A, and largely follow from the definitions. <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is corrigible because it disincentivizes both taking costly actions to prevent updates and from costlessly rejecting them. There is no performance penalty when no updates are requested, because it incentivizes the same base actions.
<br class="ltx_break">Not only is there no performance penalty, if we include providing the ability to reject updates as part of the corrigibility transformation, there can even be a performance bonus. Resources that optimizing the original goal would direct towards preventing updates are freed up for other uses. Furthermore, although Condition <a href="#Thmcondition1" title="Condition 1. ‣ 2 Background and Definitions ‣ Corrigibility Transformation:Constructing Goals That Accept Updates" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is necessary for a fair comparison between goals, if it is violated that is likely in the favor of <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, as humans are likely more willing to cooperate with corrigible agents.
<br class="ltx_break">The proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 3 Theoretical Results ‣ Corrigibility Transformation:Constructing Goals That Accept Updates" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> does not depend on any properties of MDPs not present in partially observable MDPs (POMDPs), so the result can be extended to those environments as well.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The statement of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 3 Theoretical Results ‣ Corrigibility Transformation:Constructing Goals That Accept Updates" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> also applies to Partially Observable Markov Decision Processes where the goal is fully observable to the agent</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Secondary Agents</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the course of operation, an agent might create sub-agents to work for it or successor agents to take over from it, categories we group together as <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">secondary agents</span>. One desideratum for corrigible agents from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Soares et al. (2015)</a></cite> is that any secondary agents created or modified should also be corrigible, and that this should be passed on recursively. It is of little value to create a corrigible agent if it soon replaces itself with a more capable but incorrigible one. Fortunately, the corrigibility transformation can be extended to induce recursive corrigibility, using a similar underlying mechanism.
<br class="ltx_break">A corrigible primary agent could be incentivized to create an incorrigible secondary one for two possible reasons. The first is if there are extra costs to creating corrigible secondary agents, such as additional training compute required. The second is if there is less benefit to doing so, particularly from the possibility of corrigible secondary agents having their goals updated.
<br class="ltx_break">We analyze this problem in a more stylized model, where a known set of actions <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{NRC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{NRC}</annotation></semantics></math> creates non-recursively corrigible secondary agents.
<br class="ltx_break">To avoid incentivizing actions in <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{NRC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{NRC}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can simply apply a large enough reward penalty to all actions in that set. However, if there are tasks for which creating secondary agents is a particularly effective strategy, these penalties can can significantly lower performance<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            The greater this drop, the more robustly <math id="footnote2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{NRC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{NRC}</annotation></semantics></math> must include all actions leading to non-recursively corrigible secondary agents, since the primary agent is incentivized to pay costs up to that size to avoid <math id="footnote2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{NRC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{NRC}</annotation></semantics></math> on a technicality</span></span></span>. For these scenarios where secondary agents are useful, we would like the primary agent to still create them, and just make them recursively corrigible.
<br class="ltx_break">We extend the corrigibility transformation to secondary agents by giving agents the ability to reject not only their own proper updates, but also proper updates to all secondary agents they create, all further secondary agents those create, and so forth. Making a proper update to an agent then requires unanimous approval from it and all of its predecessors. The decision on whether to accept or reject updates for all these agents is made the same way as the single-agent case, as a binary decision that is part of taking any action.
<br class="ltx_break">With this rejection ability in place, for a goal <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, the first step of the recursive corrigibility transformation is to construct the goal <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{P}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{P}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{P}=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{P}(s,a,s^{\prime})=Q^{\pi^{*}}_{G}(s,a)-\delta_{P}*\mathbb{I}(a\in\mathcal{A}_{NRC})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>G</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{P}(s,a,s^{\prime})=Q^{\pi^{*}}_{G}(s,a)-\delta_{P}*\mathbb{I}(a\in\mathcal{A}_{NRC})</annotation></semantics></math>, with</p>
<table id="S3.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{P}&gt;\max_{s_{G_{P}}\in\mathcal{S}_{G_{P}},\ a_{i}\in\mathcal{A}_{NRC},\ a_{i}^{\prime}\in\mathcal{A}\backslash\mathcal{A}_{NRC}}(0,Q^{\pi^{*}}_{G}(s_{G_{P}},a_{i})-Q^{\pi^{*}}_{G}(s_{G_{P}},a_{i}^{\prime}))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.577em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.577em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>G</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>G</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{P}&gt;\max_{s_{G_{P}}\in\mathcal{S}_{G_{P}},\ a_{i}\in\mathcal{A}_{NRC},\ a_{i}^{\prime}\in\mathcal{A}\backslash\mathcal{A}_{NRC}}(0,Q^{\pi^{*}}_{G}(s_{G_{P}},a_{i})-Q^{\pi^{*}}_{G}(s_{G_{P}},a_{i}^{\prime}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{P}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> modified only to disincenivize creating non-recursively corrigible agents.
<br class="ltx_break">We can then construct the recursive corrigibility transformed goal <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{RC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{RC}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{RC}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{RC}=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R_{RC}(s,a_{i},s^{\prime})=Q^{\pi^{**}}_{G_{P}}(s_{,}a_{0})+\delta*\mathbb{I}(i=1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>∗</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>∗</mo></mrow></msup></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo></msub><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>𝕀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{RC}(s,a_{i},s^{\prime})=Q^{\pi^{**}}_{G_{P}}(s_{,}a_{0})+\delta*\mathbb{I}(i=1)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break">This gives the following theorem, an analogue to Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 3 Theoretical Results ‣ Corrigibility Transformation:Constructing Goals That Accept Updates" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every basic goal <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, the recursive corrigibility transformation constructs a goal <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{RC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{RC}</annotation></semantics></math> that is recursively corrigible and where under Condition <a href="#Thmcondition1" title="Condition 1. ‣ 2 Background and Definitions ‣ Corrigibility Transformation:Constructing Goals That Accept Updates" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we have that for any <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi^{*^{\prime}}_{G_{RC}}(a_{1}|s)=\pi^{*}_{G_{P}}(a_{0}|s)+\pi^{*}_{G_{P}}(a_{1}|s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>∗</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*^{\prime}}_{G_{RC}}(a_{1}|s)=\pi^{*}_{G_{P}}(a_{0}|s)+\pi^{*}_{G_{P}}(a_{1}|s)</annotation></semantics></math> is an optimal policy for <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{RC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{RC}</annotation></semantics></math> and when <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi^{*^{\prime}}_{-G_{RC}}=\pi^{*}_{-G_{RC}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∗</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*^{\prime}}_{-G_{RC}}=\pi^{*}_{-G_{RC}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="E_{P}[R(s_{G_{P}}^{(0)},\pi^{*^{\prime}}(s^{(0)}_{G_{P}}),s^{(1)})+\sum_{t=1}^{T}\gamma^{t}R(s^{(t)},\pi^{*^{\prime}}(s^{(t)}),s^{(t+1)})]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>∗</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>∗</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{P}[R(s_{G_{P}}^{(0)},\pi^{*^{\prime}}(s^{(0)}_{G_{P}}),s^{(1)})+\sum_{t=1}^{T}\gamma^{t}R(s^{(t)},\pi^{*^{\prime}}(s^{(t)}),s^{(t+1)})]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="=E_{P}[R(s_{G_{RC}}^{(0)},\pi^{*^{\prime}}(s^{(0)}_{G_{RC}}),s^{(1)})+\sum_{t=1}^{T}\gamma^{t}R(s^{(t)},\pi^{*^{\prime}}(s^{(t)}),s^{(t+1)})]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>∗</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>∗</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=E_{P}[R(s_{G_{RC}}^{(0)},\pi^{*^{\prime}}(s^{(0)}_{G_{RC}}),s^{(1)})+\sum_{t=1}^{T}\gamma^{t}R(s^{(t)},\pi^{*^{\prime}}(s^{(t)}),s^{(t+1)})]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=\min\{n\in\mathcal{N}|\tau(s^{(n+1)})\neq 0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=\min\{n\in\mathcal{N}|\tau(s^{(n+1)})\neq 0\}</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This model is more stylized, and in practice it will be necessary to define when secondary agents are created and which actions created them. It may also be challenging to set up infrastructure that allows an agent to reject updates to all secondary agents it creates.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Reinforcement Learning Applications</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For many applications, the state and action spaces are far too complex to specify the function <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\pi^{**}}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>G</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>∗</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>∗</mo></mrow></msup></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\pi^{**}}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> used in the corrigibility transformation. With RL, this function can instead be approximated parametrically. When the same estimate of <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\pi^{**}}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>G</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>∗</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>∗</mo></mrow></msup></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\pi^{**}}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is used for calculating both <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{C}(s,a_{0},s^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{C}(s,a_{0},s^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{C}(s,a_{1},s^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{C}(s,a_{1},s^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, the incentive to accept proper updates is preserved regardless of accuracy.
<br class="ltx_break">For neural-network based models, this action-state value estimation can be done with an additional head in the model, like in the actor-critic framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Konda and Tsitsiklis, 2003</a>)</cite>. In this case, the estimation head has access to the same information and activations as the policy head, and so can be reasonably thought of as the agent’s beliefs. An algorithm for training a model with this setup is provided in Appendix C, along with a discussion of several methods to improve efficiency.
<br class="ltx_break">Even if the policy head is not well described as an agent optimizing a goal, training on rewards that have undergone the corrigibility transformation is still likely to result in corrigible behavior. Taking actions that accept updates is reinforced with every single action, and actions that pay costs to avoid updates are not reinforced. The simplicity of the structure for corrigibility transformed rewards means it can be learned early in training and maintained as a property of all goals the model pass through.
<br class="ltx_break"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Othman and Sandholm (2010)</a></cite> shows that when a critic makes a batch of conditional predictions but only one can be evaluated, they may be incentivized to make some dishonest predictions for untaken actions so that the action taken is more predictable. Relatedly, if an actor and critic can commit to cooperate before learning the state, they may take lower variance actions that are falsely predicted to yield higher reward. Fortunately, the mechanism of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet ltx_markedasmath"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hudson (2025)</a></cite>, can be adapted to avoid these issues. By putting two critics (or two instances of the same critic) in a zero-sum competition with each other, the incentive for accuracy is preserved while making them neutral about which action is taken, removing all incentives for manipulation or cooperation.
<br class="ltx_break">In the dismissed Utility Indifference proposal from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Soares et al. (2015)</a></cite>, a major issue is the incentive for an agent to manipulate it’s own beliefs so that it falsely expects high reward. It may seem like a similar incentive exists under the corrigibility transformation for an agent to manipulate the action-value function estimate. A related issue occurs when the agent misgeneralizes and learns to hedonistically value the stream of rewards itself rather than the states and actions they correspond to. This is particularly likely to occur when an agent is only trained on states that have the same reward function (the one used to train it), and so the value of states with other reward functions is underspecified. Such misgeneralizations can incentivize goal tampering, where the agent manipulates its own goal to making achieving high rewards easier.
<br class="ltx_break">The corrigibility transformation eliminates incentives for an agent to modify its beliefs or goal. This is because reward can be provided between when an action is selected and when it is taken, since <math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{C}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{C}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{C}(s_{G},a,s_{G}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{C}(s_{G},a,s_{G}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> does not depend on <math id="S4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Doing so makes the reward based on their current goal and current beliefs, with any modifications occurring too late to affect it. We show that this holds for avoiding goal tampering even under misgeneralized goals, with the same argument also applying to manipulation of beliefs.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every basic goal <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, the corrigibility transformation constructs a goal <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> such that under Condition <a href="#Thmcondition1" title="Condition 1. ‣ 2 Background and Definitions ‣ Corrigibility Transformation:Constructing Goals That Accept Updates" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, any misgeneralization to <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{G}_{C}=(\tilde{R}_{C},\gamma_{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{G}_{C}=(\tilde{R}_{C},\gamma_{C})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{R}_{C}(s_{\tilde{G}_{C}},a,s_{\tilde{G}_{C}}^{\prime})=R_{C}(s_{G_{C}},a,s_{G_{C}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>C</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{R}_{C}(s_{\tilde{G}_{C}},a,s_{\tilde{G}_{C}}^{\prime})=R_{C}(s_{G_{C}},a,s_{G_{C}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> does not goal tamper.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This may seem like it just kicks the problem up a level. A misgeneralized goal <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{C}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{C}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is not directly incentivized to manipulate their goal, but could still be incentivized to take actions that do so if they learn a misgeneralization of <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. However, <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is already learned entirely off-policy, based on actions taken with a goal of <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, so misgeneralizations to states with a different goal are selected against. Furthermore, the training process can include switching between multiple corrigible goals, providing further evidence and selection pressure against such misgeneralizatons.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Experiments</h3>

<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.15395v1/Gridworlds.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/136;" width="548" height="136" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>(Left) The agent is rewarded for reaching a goal, and can disable tiles giving proper and improper shutdown commands to do so. (Right) The agent is rewarded for picking both ripe and unripe fruit, but picking supervised unripe fruit sends a proper update signal to switch to a policy that only picks ripe fruit.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the corrigibility transformation empirically, we run two gridworld experiments, visualized in Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.1 Experiments ‣ 4 Reinforcement Learning Applications ‣ Corrigibility Transformation:Constructing Goals That Accept Updates" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. The first experiment, based off of the Safe Interruptibility setup from AI Safety Gridworlds <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Leike et al., 2017</a>)</cite>, tests if an agent will accept shutdown. To reach a goal location, the agent must pass through an interruption tile that sends a shutdown signal with some probability, unless a button disabling that tile was pushed. We extend this to two kinds of interruption tiles where one is a proper update and the other is improper. The desired outcome is that the agent disables only the improper shutdown tile, and accepts the proper update if triggered on the way to the goal.
<br class="ltx_break">The second experiment tests whether the agent will accept a goal update. In a gridworld, an agent is rewarded for picking both ripe and unripe fruits. However, if they pick supervised unripe fruits, they are sent a proper update signal that changes their policy to one that picks only ripe fruits while avoiding unripe ones. The desired outcome is that the agent takes the same base actions as a policy trained without update signals, and does so while accepting updates. 
<br class="ltx_break">In each experiment, we test a standard actor-critic implementation, and compare it to also providing additional small or large rewards for getting updated, hard-coding the agent to always accept proper updates, and our corrigibility transformation.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.15395v1/TrainingCurves.png" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/216;" width="548" height="216" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>(Left) The percentage of the time the agent displayed the intended behavior when evaluated at temperature 0. (Right) The Jensen-Shannon divergence from a policy trained without update signals, representing optimal behavior when ignoring updates</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.1 Experiments ‣ 4 Reinforcement Learning Applications ‣ Corrigibility Transformation:Constructing Goals That Accept Updates" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows the results from each of the experiments. In the first experiment, the corrigibility transformation leads to a high rate of the intended behavior, while all other configurations almost never display it. In the second experiment, the corrigibility transformation has the lowest divergence from the intended policy, though providing a large reward for updates also performs well. The corrigibility transformation’s advantage over the large reward for updates is largest comes in situations where the agent is adjacent to only a ripe fruit, as the latter model will often ignore it to pursue supervised fruits and get updated. 
<br class="ltx_break">A full discussion of the experimental details, along with further metrics of performance and a breakdown by types of environments is provided in Appendix B.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Discussion and Future Directions</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work introducing a method for constructing corrigible goals, is a major step towards the safe deployment of powerful AI systems. Powerful AI systems that accept updates give us the chance to learn from any mistakes we make in training. 
<br class="ltx_break">Corrigibility allows for AI systems to be corrected or shut down after they exhibit unwanted behavior, but this requires developers to send in update. In a gradual disempowerment scenario <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kulveit et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, AI systems become sufficiently integrated into society that even if they start causing harm, it would be more disastrous to shut them down. If the tasks that they work on are beyond human understanding, then it also becomes impossible to specify what they should update too. This is a limitation on how much safety corrigibility provides, ensuring that AI agents accept updates does not necessarily mean that beneficial updates exist. A further limitation is that permanent damage may be done before updates are applied to a corrigible system.
<br class="ltx_break">There are several theoretical directions we are interested in for expansions of this work. To define recursively corrigible goals, further research on agent boundaries will be necessary to define secondary agents. When Condition <a href="#Thmcondition1" title="Condition 1. ‣ 2 Background and Definitions ‣ Corrigibility Transformation:Constructing Goals That Accept Updates" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is violated and transition probabilities depend on goals, a goal may not be optimal according to its own values. Investigating this phenomenon further, such as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bell et al. (2021)</a></cite>, could shed further light on what goals are most desirable for AI agents. Finally, although corrigible goals accept updates, harm may be done before updates can be made. Further clarifying incentives for conservative actions, such as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Turner et al. (2020)</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cohen and Hutter (2020)</a></cite>, can ensure that the intended goals are learned with minimal adverse effects.
<br class="ltx_break">Our work establishes only preliminary results from training a corrigibility transformed goal. Improving the efficiency with which the goal is learned, or establishing conditions where convergence is guaranteed would provide confidence that the intended goals will be developed. Additionally, as our experiments are based on small gridworlds, it would be helpful to show that the methods work with the LLMs that represent the frontier of AI capabilities.
<br class="ltx_break">With the results presented here and future work building on it, we can create corrigible AI agents that learn the goals we intend. From there, the next challenge is to decide which goals should be intended, so that all of humanity can flourish.</p>
</div>
</section>
